          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            is
            %strong connected
            if it can not be split up into two independent parts.
          %p
            Every topological space may be decomposed into disjoint maximal connected
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspaces"} subspaces
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+components"} connected components
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying"} underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            of a topological space is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
            of the underlying sets of its connected components, but the space itself is not necessarily the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
            of its connected components in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            of topological spaces.
          %p
            One often studies topological ideas first for connected spaces and then generalises to general spaces. This is especially true if one is studying such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nice+topological+spaces"} nice topological spaces
            that every space
            %em is
            a coproduct of connected components (such as for example
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+connected+topological+spaces"} locally connected topological spaces
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %h3#ClassicalDefinition Classical definition
          #ConnectedSpace.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              %strong (connected topological space)
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi τ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \tau)
              is
              %em connected
              if the following equivalent conditions hold:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  For all
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pairs"} pairs
                  of topological spaces
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi τ
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 2
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi τ
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_1, \tau_1), (X_2, \tau_2)
                  such that
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi τ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \tau)
                  is
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphic
                  to their
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union+space"} disjoint union space
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi τ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo ≃
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi τ
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊔
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 2
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi τ
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo ,
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        (X,\tau)
                        \\,\simeq\,
                        (X_1,\tau_1)
                        \\sqcup
                        (X_2,\tau_2)
                        \\,,
                %p
                  exactly one of the two spaces is the
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+space"} empty space
              %li
                %p
                  For all
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pairs"} pairs
                  of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ⊂
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1, U_2 \subset X
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ∪
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mn 2
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo =
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi X
                        %mphantom
                          %mi A
                        %mtext and
                        %mphantom
                          %mi A
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ∩
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mn 2
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo =
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi ∅
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        U_1
                        \\cup
                        U_2
                        \\,=\,
                        X
                        \\phantom{A}\text{and} \phantom{A}
                        U_1 \cap U_2
                        \\,=\,
                        \\emptyset
                %p
                  then exactly one of the two subsets is the
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty set
              %li
                %p
                  If a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi CO
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CO \subseteq X
                  is
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/clopen+set"} clopen
                  (both
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subset"} closed
                  and
                  = succeed ")," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subset"} open
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi CO
                        %mo =
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CO = X
                  if and only if
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi CO
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CO
                  is
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited
          #ConnectedEmptySpace.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              According to def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ConnectedSpace"}
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+topological+space"} empty topological space
              is not regarded as connected. Some authors do want the empty space to count as a connected space. This means to change in the two first items of def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ConnectedSpace"}
              the “exactly one” to “at least one” and in the third item “if and only if” to “if”.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              The conditions in def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ConnectedSpace"}
              are indeed equivalent.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p First consider the equivalence of the first two statements:
            %p
              Suppose that in every disjoint union decomposition of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi τ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\tau)
              exactly one summand is empty. Now consider two disjoint open subsets
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1, U_2 \subset X
              whose union is
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and whose intersection is empty. We need to show that exactly one of the two subsets is empty.
            %p
              Write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi τ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (U_1, \tau_{1})
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi τ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (U_2, \tau_2)
              for the corresponding
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+subspaces"} topological subspaces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspace+topology"} subspace topology
              and the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union+space"} disjoint union space
              we have a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi τ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi τ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    X \simeq (U_1, \tau_1) \sqcup (U_2, \tau_2)
            %p
              because by assumption every open subset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset X
              is the disjoint union of open subsets of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_2
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo =
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∩
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⊔
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∩
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∩
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    U = U \cap X = U \cap (U_1 \sqcup U_2) = (U \cap U_1) \sqcup (U \cap U_2)
                    \\,,
            %p which is the definition of the disjoint union topology.
            %p
              Hence by assumption exactly one of the two summand spaces is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+space"} empty space
              and hence the underlying set is the empty set.
            %p
              Conversely, suppose that for every pair of open subsets
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1, U_2 \subset U
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∪
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1 \cup U_2 = X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∩
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mi ∅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1 \cap U_2 = \emptyset
              then exactly one of the two is empty. Now consider a homeomorphism of the form
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi τ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi τ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊔
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi τ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\tau) \simeq (X_1, \tau_1) \sqcup (X_2,\tau_2)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union+space"} disjoint union space
              this means that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1, X_2 \subset X
              are disjoint open subsets of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              which cover
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %p Now regarding the equivalence to the third statement:
            %p
              If a subset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CO
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CO \subset X
              is both closed and open, this means equivalently that it is open and that its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complement
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∖
                    %mi CO
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \setminus CO
              is also open, hence equivalently that there are two open subsets
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CO
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∖
                    %mi CO
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CO, X \setminus CO \subset X
              whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/union"} union
              is
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection"} intersection
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+space"} empty
          %h3#ConstructiveDefinition Constructive definition
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            the conditions in Def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ConnectedSpace"}
            no longer need to be equivalent. See
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Taylor10"} Taylor 2010, Def. 13.2
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ConnectedSpace"}
            and of “overt connectedness” for the second.
          %p
            Related discussion in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} cohesive homotopy type theory
            (and for the special case of the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+real+numbers"} Dedekind real numbers
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Shulman18"} Shulman 2018, Thm. 11.1, 11.3
          %p
            In this language of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            one might then say:
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-truncated"} 0-truncated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+infinity-groupoid"} cohesive infinity-groupoid
            is “compact connected” (in the sense of
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Taylor10"} Taylor 2010, Def. 13.2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspaces"} subspaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq S
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \subseteq S
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/embeddings"} embeddings
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_{A,S}:A \hookrightarrow S
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo ,
                      %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %mi B
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_{B,S}:B \hookrightarrow S
            such that the embedding
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∪
                      %mi B
                      %mo ,
                      %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∪
                  %mi B
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_{A \cup B,S}:A \cup B \hookrightarrow S
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+in+homotopy+type+theory"} equivalence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∪
                      %mi B
                      %mo ,
                      %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∪
                  %mi B
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (i_{A \cup B,S}:A \cup B \simeq S)
            and the embedding
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mi ∅
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∩
                      %mi B
                  %mo :
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_{\emptyset,A \cap B}:\emptyset \hookrightarrow A \cap B
            is an equivalence
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mrow
                        %mi ∅
                        %mo ,
                        %mi A
                        %mo ∩
                        %mi B
                    %mo :
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (i_{\emptyset,A \cap B}:\emptyset \simeq A \cap B)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_{A,S}
            is an equivalence (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_{A,S}:A \simeq S
            ) or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo ,
                      %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_{B,S}
            is an equivalence (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo ,
                      %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %mi B
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_{B,S}:B \simeq S
            ).
          %h3#categorytheoretic_definition Category-theoretic definition
          %p
            In the language of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ConnectedSpace"}
            may be rephrased as follows:
          %p
            Let
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            denote the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of all
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
          %p
            Then a topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is connected precisely if the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Top
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  hom(X, -)
                  \\;\colon\;
                  Top \longrightarrow Set
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preserved+limit"} preserves
            all
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} coproducts
          %p
            This, in turn, is equivalent to just requiring that the functor preserves binary coproducts (a detailed proof of this, in a more general setting, is given at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+object"} connected object
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "connected+object#RespectForBinaryCoproductsIsSufficient"} this proposition
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo +
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  hom(X,Y)
                  +
                  hom(X,Z)
                  \\longrightarrow
                  hom(X,Y + Z)
                  ,\,
          %p
            so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is connected if this is always a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+objects"} connected objects
            in an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensive+category"} extensive category
          %p
            The variant of the definition according to remark
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ConnectedEmptySpace"}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo +
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom(X,Y) + hom(X,Z) \to hom(X,Y + Z)
          %p
            be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjections"} surjections
          %p
            However, many results come out more cleanly by disqualifying the empty space as connected (much as one disqualifies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            when one defines the notion of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+number"} prime number
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+space"} empty space
            and at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/too+simple+to+be+simple"} too simple to be simple
          %h3#connected_components Connected components
          %p
            Every topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            admits an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \sim y
            means that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            belong to some subspace which is connected. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+class"} equivalence class
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Conn
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Conn(x)
            of an element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is thus the union of all connected subspaces containing
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#2"}
            that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Conn
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Conn(x)
            is itself connected. It is called the
            %strong connected component
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#5"}
          #ComponentsConnected.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              %strong (connected components)
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi τ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\tau)
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+components"} connected components
              are the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+classes"} equivalence classes
              under the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              which regards two points as equivalent if they both sit in some
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subset"} open subset
              which, as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+subspace"} topological subspace
              (example
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SubspaceTopology"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+topological+space"} connected
              (def.
              = succeed "):" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ConnectedTopologicalSpace"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %munder
                        %mo ∃
                        %mrow
                          %mi U
                          %mo ⊂
                          %mi X
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mtext \open
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mi x
                          %mo ,
                          %mi y
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi U
                          %mo )
                        %mphantom
                          %mi A
                        %mtext and
                        %mphantom
                          %mi A
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mi U
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mtext is connected
                          %mo )
                        %mo )
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (x \sim y)
                    \\;\coloneqq\;
                    \\left(
                    \\underset{ U \subset X \; \text{\open} }{\exists}
                    \\left(
                    \\left( x,y \in U \right)
                    \\phantom{A}\text{and}\phantom{A}
                    \\left( U \, \text{is connected} \right)
                    \\right)
                    \\right)
                    \\,.
          #QuasiComponentsConnected.num_defn
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              %strong (quasi-components)
            %p
              There is another
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∼
                      %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim_q
              modelling a notion of connected components, where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %msub
                      %mo ∼
                      %mi q
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \sim_q y
              iff
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x) = f(y)
              for every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuous map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon X \to D
              to a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+space"} discrete space
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %p
              The corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+class"} equivalence class
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              may alternatively be described as the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection"} intersection
              of all
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/clopen+set"} clopen subsets
              that contain
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              %strong quasi-component
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi QConn
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} QConn(x)
            %p
              It is easy to prove that this relates to the equivalence classes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Conn
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Conn(x)
              from Def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ComponentsConnected"}
              as
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Conn
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi QConn
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Conn(x) \subseteq QConn(x)
            %p
              and that equality holds if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+Hausdorff+space"} compact Hausdorff
              or is
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+connected+topological+space"} locally connected
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
              as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+subspace"} subspace
              of the
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
          #comb.num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              For an example where
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Conn
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≠
                      %mi QConn
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Conn(x) \neq QConn(x)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              to be the following
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspace"} subspace
              of
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ×
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0, 1] \times [0, 1]
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ∪
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = \{(0, 0), (0, 1)\} \cup \bigcup_{n \geq 1} \{1/n\} \times [0, 1]
            %p
              In this example,
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Conn
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Conn((0, 1)) = \{(0, 1)\}
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi QConn
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} QConn((0, 1)) = \{(0, 0), (0, 1)\}
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              %strong Warning
            %p
              It is not generally true that a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union+space"} disjoint union space
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
              in
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+components"} connected components
            %p
              The spaces such that all their open subspaces are the disjoint union of their connected components are the
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+connected+topological+spaces"} locally connected topological spaces
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              The connected components in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cantor+space"} Cantor space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^{\mathbb{N}}
              (with its topology as a product of 2-point discrete spaces) are just the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singletons"} singletons
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+topology"} discrete topology
            %p
              Similarly for set of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
              with its absolute-value topology (the one induced as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+subspace"} topological subspace
              of the
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#basic Basic examples
          #ContinuousImagesOfConnectedSpacesAreConnected.num_example
            %h6#example_3 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/images"} images
                of connected spaces are connected)
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+monomorphism"} regular
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
              of a connected space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              under a continuous map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X \to Y
              (i.e., the set-theoretic image with the subspace topology inherited from
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is connected and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X \to Y
              is
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epic
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              is connected:
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be a connected topological space, let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              be any
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f \;\colon\; X \longrightarrow Y
            %p
              be a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi X
                    %munderover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi surjective
                      %mi p
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %munderover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi injective
                      %mi i
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f \;\colon\; X \underoverset{surjective}{p}{\longrightarrow} f(X) \underoverset{injective}{i}{\longrightarrow} Y
            %p
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(X)
              equipped either with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspace+topology"} subspace topology
              relative to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              or the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+topology"} quotient topology
              relative to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a connected topological space, then so is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(X)
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1,U_2 \subset f(X)
              be two
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∪
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1 \cup U_2 = f(X)
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ∩
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %mi ∅
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1 \cap U_2 = \emptyset
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty set
            %p
              Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pre-images"} pre-images
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi p
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi p
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^{-1}(U_1), p^{-1}(U_2) \subset X
              are open subsets and are still disjoint. Since preimages preserve unions it also follows that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi p
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∪
                      %msup
                        %mi p
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^{-1}(U_1) \cup p^{-1}(U_2) = X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is connected, it follows that one of these two pre-images
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi p
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^{-1}(U_i)
              is the empty set. Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjective+function"} surjective
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
              is empty, which means that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(X)
              is connected.
          #2.num_example
            %h6#example_4 Example
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} Wide pushouts
              of connected spaces are connected. (This would of course be false if the empty space were considered to be connected.) This follows from the hom-functor definition of connectedness, plus the fact that coproducts in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              commute with
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wide+pullbacks"} wide pullbacks
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+limit"} connected colimits
              of connected spaces are connected.
          #5.num_example
            %h6#example_5 Example
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subseteq X
              is a connected subspace and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi T
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mover
                      %mi S
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subseteq T \subseteq \overline{S}
              (i.e. if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is between
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              and its closure), then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is connected. Or, what is essentially the same: if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              has a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+subspace"} dense
              connected subspace
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is connected.
          %p
            (see also prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ClosureOfConnectedSubspaceIsConnected"}
            below)
          #ProductSpaceOfConnectedSpacesIsConnected.num_example
            %h6#example_6 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+space"} product space
                of connected spaces is connected)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{X_i\}_{i \in I}
              be a set of connected spaces. Then also their
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+topological+space"} product topological space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{i \in I}{\prod}X_i
              (with the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tychonoff+topology"} Tychonoff topology
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              This relies on some special features of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+object"} connected object
              in
              %a{:href => "connected+object#prod"} this theorem
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "connected+object#prod2"} this remark
            %p
              Here is an alternative elementary proof in
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point-set+topology"} point-set topology
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⊂
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1, U_2 \subset \underset{i \in I}{\prod}X_i
              be an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+cover"} open cover
              of the product space by two disjoint open subsets. We need to show that precisely one of the two is empty. Since each
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_i
              is connected and hence non-empty, the product space is not empty, and hence it is sufficient to show that at lest one of the two is empty.
            %p
              Assume on the contrary that both
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_2
              are non-empty.
            %p
              Observe first that if so, then we could find
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1 \in U_1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_2 \in U_2
              whose coordinates differed only a a finite subset of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tychonoff+topology"} Tychonoff topology
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_i(U_1) = X_i
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_i(U_2) = X_i
              for all but a finite number of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi iI
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \in iI
            %p
              Next observe that we then could even find
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %msub
                      %mo ′
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x'_1 \in U_1
              that differed only in a single coordinate from
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1
              differs from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_2
              and change it to the corresponding coordinate of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_2
              are a cover, the resulting point is either in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1
              or in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_2
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1
              already differed in only one coordinate from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_2
              and we may take
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %msub
                        %mo ′
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ≔
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x'_1 \coloneqq x_1
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1
              and repeat the argument. By
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} induction
              this finally yields an
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %msub
                      %mo ′
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x'_1
              as claimed.
            %p
              Therefore it is now sufficient to see that it leads to a contradiction to assume that there are points
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1 \in U_1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_2 \in U_2
              that differ in only the
              = succeed "th" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_0 \in I
              then
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1 = x_2
            %p Observe that the inclusion
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ι
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi i
                          %mn 0
                    %mo ⟶
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\iota \colon X_{i_0} \longrightarrow \underset{i \in I}{\prod} X_i
            %p
              which is the identity on the
              = succeed "th" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_0
              = succeed "th" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1
              or equivalently of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_2
              is a continuous function, by the nature of the Tychonoff topology.
            %p
              Therefore also the restrictions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota^{-1}(U_1)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota^{-1}(U_2)
              are open subsets. Moreover they are still disjoint and cover
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_i
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_i
              = succeed "-component" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_2
              must be in the other subset of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_i
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_2
              must both be in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1
              or both in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_2
          #ConnectedSubspacesOfRealLineAreTheIntervals.num_example
            %h6#example_7 Example
            %p
              %strong (connected subspaces of the real line are the intervals)
            %p
              Regard the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
              with its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+topology"} metric topology
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspace"} subspace
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subset \mathbb{R}
              is connected (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ConnectedSpace"}
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mo ∀
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi y
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi S
                        %mo ⊂
                        %mi ℝ
                    %munder
                      %mo ∀
                      %mrow
                        %mi r
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mi x
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi r
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi y
                        %mo )
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mi r
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi S
                        %mo )
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\underset{x,y \in S \subset \mathbb{R}}{\forall}
                    \\underset{ r \in \mathbb{R}  }{\forall}
                    \\left(
                    \\left(
                    x \lt r \lt y
                    \\right)
                    \\Rightarrow
                    \\left(
                    r \in S
                    \\right)
                    \\right)
                    \\,.
            %p
              In particular for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi I
                      %mi i
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi ℝ
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ I_i \subset \mathbb{R} \}_{i \in I}
              a set of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+subset"} disjoint
              intervals, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              is the set of connected components of the union
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mo ∪
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi I
                      %msub
                        %mi I
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{i \in I}{\cup} I_i
          .proof
            %h6#proof_4 Proof
            %p
              Suppose on the contrary that we have
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi r
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \lt r \lt y
              but
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi r
                      %mo ∉
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \notin S
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspace+topology"} subspace topology
              there would be a decomposition of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+subset"} disjoint
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mi S
                      %mo ∩
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi r
                      %mo ,
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo )
                    %mo ⊔
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mi S
                      %mo ∩
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mi r
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    S
                    \=
                    \\left(
                    S \cap (r,\infty)
                    \\right)
                    \\sqcup
                    \\left(
                    S \cap (-\infty,r)
                    \\right)
                    \\,.
            %p
              But since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \lt r
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \lt y
              both these open subsets were
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} non-empty
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              is connected. This yields a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+by+contradiction"} proof by contradiction
          %h3#exotic_examples Exotic examples
          %p
            The
            %a{:href => "basic"} basic results
            above give a plethora of ways to construct connected spaces. More exotic examples are sometimes useful, especially for constructing counterexamples.
          .num_example
            %h6#example_8 Example
            %p
              The following, due to
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#Bing"} Bing
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+space"} Hausdorff space
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℚ
                      %mo ×
                      %mi ℚ
                      %mo :
                      %mi y
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q = \{(x, y) \in \mathbb{Q} \times \mathbb{Q}: y \geq 0\}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mo ,
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_{\epsilon, a, b}
              for each point
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a, b) \in Q
              and
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon \gt 0
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mi b
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ∪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Q
                    %mo :
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi a
                      %mo +
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi θ
                      %mo −
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ∪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Q
                    %mo :
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi a
                      %mo −
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi θ
                      %mo −
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_{a, b} \coloneqq \{(a, b)\} \cup \{(s, 0) \in Q: {|a + b/\theta - s|} \lt \epsilon\} \cup \{(s, 0) \in Q: {|a - b/\theta - s|} \lt \epsilon\}
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta \lt 0
              is some chosen fixed irrational number. It is easy to see this space is Hausdorff (using the fact that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
              is irrational). However, the
              %em closure
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mo ,
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_{\epsilon, a, b}
              consists of points
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x, y)
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q \times Q
              with either
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo −
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo −
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo −
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi θ
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo −
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %mi ϵ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x-a) - \epsilon \leq (y-b)/\theta \leq (x-a) + \epsilon
              or
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo −
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo −
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mo ≤
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo −
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi θ
                      %mo ≤
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo −
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                      %mi ϵ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x-a) - \epsilon \leq -(y-b)/\theta \leq (x-a) + \epsilon
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mi ϵ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2\epsilon
              and slopes
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi θ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi θ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -\theta
          .num_example
            %h6#example_9 Example
            %p
              This example is due to
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Golomb"} Golomb
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
              by taking a basis to consist of sets
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                          %mo ,
                          %mi b
                      %mo ≔
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi a
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{a,b} \coloneqq \{a k + b | k = 1,2, \ldots\}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b \in \mathbb{N}
              are relatively prime. The space is Hausdorff, but the intersection of the closures of two non-empty open sets is never empty, so this space is connected.
          %h2#properties Properties
          #LocallyConstantFunctionsOnConnectedSpaces.num_example
            %h6#example_10 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+constant+function"} locally constant function
                on
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+topological+spaces"} connected topological spaces
                are
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constant+functions"} constant functions
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a connected topological space and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon X \to Y
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+constant+function"} locally constant
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is in fact a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constant+function"} constant function
          .proof
            %h6#proof_5 Proof
            %p
              By definition of locally constant functions, every point
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
              has an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+neighborhood"} open neighborhood
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_x
              such that the restriction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\vert_{U_x}
              is a constant function. The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unions"} unions
              of these neighborhoods for a fixed constant value hence are disjoint open subsets that constitute a cover of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is constant.
          %h2#pathconnectedness Path-connectedness
          %p An important variation on the theme of connectedness is path-connectedness.
          #TopologicalSpacePath.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              %strong (continuous path in a topological space)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi τ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\tau)
              be a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              %em continuous path
              (or just
              = succeed "," do
                %em path
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi τ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\tau)
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
              of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi γ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi τ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\gamma
                    \\;\colon\;
                    [0,1]
                    \\longrightarrow
                    (X,\tau)
            %p
              where the domain is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+interval"} closed interval
              equipped with its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspace+topology"} subspace topology
            %p
              One says that the path connects the point
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma(0) \in X
              with the point
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma(1) \in X
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
              a fixed point, then the subset
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi PConn
                      %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %mo {
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %munder
                        %mo ∃
                        %mrow
                          %mtext path
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mi γ
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mi γ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 0
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mi x
                          %mo )
                        %mphantom
                          %mrow
                        %mtext and
                        %mphantom
                          %mi A
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mi γ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mi y
                          %mo )
                        %mo )
                      %mo }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    PConn_x(X)
                    \\;\coloneqq\;
                    \\left\{
                    y \in X
                    \\;\vert\;
                    \\underset{\text{path}\, \gamma}{\exists}
                    \\left(
                    \\left(
                    \\gamma(0) = x
                    \\right)
                    \\phantom{}
                    \\text{and}
                    \\phantom{A}
                    \\left(
                    \\gamma(1) = y
                    \\right)
                    \\right)
                    \\right\}
            %p
              is called the
              %em path-connected component
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %p
              The set of path connected components of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is denoted
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\pi_0(X)
                    \\,.
          %p
            The set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0(X)
            of path components (the 0th “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+group"}> homotopy group
            ”) is thus the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequalizer"} coequalizer
            in
          #eq:ConnectedComponentsAsCoequalizer.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %munderover
                    %mo ⇉
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ev
                        %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ev
                        %mn 0
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\hom([0, 1], X)
                  \\underoverset
                  {ev_1}
                  {ev_0}
                  {\rightrightarrows}
                  \\hom(1, X) \to \pi_0(X) .
          %p
            Observe that this is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+coequalizer"} reflexive coequalizer
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo !
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(!, X): \hom(1, X) \to \hom([0, 1], X)
          %p
            (We can even topologize
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0(X)
            by taking the coequalizer in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            of
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mover
                    %munder
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ev
                          %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ev
                          %mn 0
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{[0, 1]} \stackrel{\overset{ev_0}{\to}}{\underset{ev_1}{\to}} X,
          %p
            taking advantage of the fact that the locally compact Hausdorff space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0, 1]
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/exponential+law+for+spaces"} exponentiable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+space"} discrete
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+path-connected+space"} locally path-connected
          %p
            We say
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is
            %strong path-connected
            if it has exactly one path component.
          %p
            It follows easily from the basic results
            %a{:href => "basic"} above
            that:
          #ConnectedPathConnectedSpace.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              A path connected space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is connected.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_6 Proof
            %p
              Assume it were not, then it would be covered by two disjoint inhabited open subsets
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1, U_2 \subset X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi γ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma \colon [0,1] \to X
              from a point in one of the open subsets to a point in the other. The continuity would imply that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi γ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi γ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma^{-1}(U_1), \gamma^{-1}(U_2) \subset [0,1]
              were a disjoint open cover of the interval. This would be in contradiction to the fact that intervals are connected. Hence we have a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+by+contradiction"} proof by contradiction
          %p
            However, it need not be closed (and therefore need not be the connected component of
            = succeed ");" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+path-connected+space"} locally path-connected
          #sine.num_example
            %h6#example_11 Example
            %p
              The
              %strong topologist’s sine curve
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ∧
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi y
                    %mo =
                    %mi sin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ∨
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ∧
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi y
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ (x, y) \in \mathbb{R}^2 \;:\; (0 \lt x \leq 1 \;\wedge\; y = sin(1/x)) \;\vee\; (0 = x \;\wedge\; -1 \leq y \leq 1) \}
            %p
              provides a classic example where the path component of a point need not be closed. (Specifically, consider a point on the locus of
              = succeed ".)" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo =
                      %mi sin
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y = \sin(1/x)
          %p
            The basic categorical Results
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ContinuousImagesOfConnectedSpacesAreConnected"}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#2"}
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ProductSpaceOfConnectedSpacesIsConnected"}
            above carry over upon replacing “connected” by “path-connected”. (As of course does example
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ConnectedSubspacesOfRealLineAreTheIntervals"}
          %p
            Finally, as a contrast to a path-connected space, a
            %strong totally path-disconnected
            space is a space such that its set of path components is equal to the underlying set of the space. Equivalently, that there are no non-constant paths. This by far does not mean that the space is discrete!
          %h3#arcconnectedness Arc-connectedness
          %p A refinement of the notion of path-connected space is that of arc-connected (or arcwise-connected) space:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              A space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is
              %strong arc-connected
              if for any two distinct
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y \in X
              there exists an
              %em injective
              continuous map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo :
                    %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha: I \to X
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha(0) = x
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha(1) = y
          %p
            Arc-connected spaces are of course path-connected, but there are trivial examples (using an
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indiscrete+topology"} indiscrete topology
          #arc.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              A path-connected
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+space"} Hausdorff space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is arc-connected.
          %p
            This immediately generalizes to the statement that in a Hausdorff space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo :
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha: I \to X
            can be connected by an arc: just apply the theorem to the image
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha(I)
          %p
            For a proof of this theorem, see
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Willard"} Willard
            = succeed "," do
              %em Peano space
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+topological+space"} connected
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+connected+topological+space"} locally connected
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metrizable+space"} metrizable space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha(I) \subseteq X
            of a path is in fact a Peano space, so that the path
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo :
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha: I \to \alpha(I)
            can be replaced by an arc.
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_2 Lemma
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is Hausdorff and there is a continuous surjection
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi I
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: I \to X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peano+space"} Peano space
          .proof
            %h6#proof_7 Proof
            %p
              Obviously
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is compact (Hausdorff) and connected.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+space"} quotient space
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is a closed surjection (using compactness of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              and Hausdorffness of
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is locally connected by
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/locally+connected+topological+space#quot"} this lemma
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separation+axiom"} regular
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urysohn+metrization+theorem"} Urysohn metrization theorem
              to show
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is second-countable.
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
              be a countable base for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              be the collection consisting of finite unions of elements of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℬ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_f(\mathcal{C}) \coloneqq \{\forall_f(C) = \neg f(\neg C): C \in \mathcal{C}\}
              is an (evidently countable) base for
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subseteq X
              is open and
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}(p)
              is compact, so there exist finitely many
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_1, \ldots, B_n \in \mathcal{B}
              with
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊆
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∪
                    %mi …
                    %mo ∪
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi n
                    %mo ⊆
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}(p) \subseteq B_1 \cup \ldots \cup B_n \subseteq f^{-1}(U).
            %p
              Put
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ∪
                      %mi …
                      %mo ∪
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C = B_1 \cup \ldots \cup B_n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in \forall_f(C)
              by the adjunction
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo ⊣
                      %msub
                        %mo ∀
                        %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1} \dashv \forall_f
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∀
                        %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊆
                      %msub
                        %mo ∀
                        %mi f
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_f(C) \subseteq \forall_f f^{-1}(U) = U
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∀
                        %mi f
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %mi id
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_f f^{-1} = id
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∃
                        %mi f
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %mi id
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_f f^{-1} = id
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_f(\mathcal{C})
              is a base.
          %p
            The converse of this lemma is the celebrated
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hahn-Mazurkiewicz+theorem"} Hahn-Mazurkiewicz theorem
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be a nonempty Hausdorff space. Then there exists a continuous surjection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha: [0, 1] \to X
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a Peano space. In particular, a nonempty Peano space is path-connected.
          %p (The terminology “Peano space” is given in recognition of Peano’s discovery of space-filling curves, as for example the unit square.)
          %h3#pathcomponents_functor Path-components functor
          %p
            As above, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Top
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0 \colon Top \to Set
            be the functor which assigns to each space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            its set of path components
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0(X)
          .un_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              The functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Top
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0 \colon Top \to Set
              preserves arbitrary products.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_8 Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_i
              be a family of spaces; we must show that the comparison map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0(\prod_i X_i) \to \prod_i \pi_0(X_i)
            %p
              is invertible. Injectivity: suppose
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_i), (y_i) \in \prod_i X_i
              are tuples that map to the same tuple of path-components
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (c_i)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_i)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (y_i)
              belong to the same path component. For each
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_i
              belong to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_i
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_i: I \to X_i
              connecting
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_i
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \alpha_i \rangle \colon I \to \prod_i X_i
              connects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_i)
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (y_i)
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (c_i) \in \prod_i \pi_0(X_i)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_i
              is nonempty for each
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i
              therein. Then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_i)
              maps to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (c_i)
          %p
            An elegant proof of the previous proposition but for preservation of
            %em finite
            products is as follows: both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(I, -)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(1, -)
            preserve products, and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+coequalizer"} reflexive coequalizer
            of product-preserving functors
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to Set
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sifted+colimit"} sifted colimit
          .un_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              The functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Top
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0 \colon Top \to Set
              preserves arbitrary coproducts.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_9 Proof
            %p
              The functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi I
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Top
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(I, -) \colon Top \to Set
              preserves coproducts since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              is connected, and similarly for
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(1, -)
          %h3#pseudoarcs Pseudo-arcs
          %p Point-set topology is filled with counterexamples. An unusual type of example is that of pseudo-arc:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em pseudo-arc
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum"} metric continuum
              with more than one point such that every subcontinuum (a subspace that is a continuum) cannot be expressed as a union of two proper subcontinua.
          %p
            A pseudo-arc
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is necessarily totally path-disconnected: two distinct points
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            cannot be connected by a path in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#arc"}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha: [0, 1] \to X
            be an arc from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha([0, 1])
            is a union of proper subcontinua
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha([0, 1/2])
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha([1/2, 1])
          %p
            Remarkably, all pseudo-arcs are homeomorphic, and a pseudo-arc is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homogeneous+space"} homogeneous space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+cube"} Hilbert cube
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            (or in any Euclidean space) is a dense
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_\delta
            set (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-delta+set"} G-delta set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Polish+space"} Polish space
            of all nonempty compact subsets of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            under the
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+metric"} Hausdorff metric
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Bing2"} Bing2
          %p
            A typical way in which pseudo-arcs arise is through
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+limits"} inverse limits
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamical+systems"} dynamical systems
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_3 Theorem
            %p
              There is a
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty
              function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: I \to I
              such that the limit of the diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi …
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi f
                    %mi I
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi f
                    %mi I
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi f
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ldots \stackrel{f}{\to} I \stackrel{f}{\to} I \stackrel{f}{\to} I
            %p is a pseudo-arc.
          %p
            Roughly speaking, Henderson’s
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is a small “notched” perturbation of the squaring function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo :
                    %mi x
                    %mo ↦
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0, 1] \to [0, 1]: x \mapsto x^2
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.akasimikrasna.sk/uploads/1/4/1/0/14106449/spitalsky2014.pdf"} here
          %h2#Properties Properties
          .num_prop
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intermediate+value+theorem"}> intermediate value theorem
                )
            %p
              Regard the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              with their
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+topology"} metric topology
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+interval"} closed interval
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [a,b] \subset \mathbb{R}
              equipped with its
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspace+topology"} subspace topology
            %p
              Then a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f \colon [a,b] \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}
            %p
              takes every value in between
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(a)
              and f(b).
          .proof
            %h6#proof_10 Proof
            %p
              By example
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ConnectedSubspacesOfRealLineAreTheIntervals"}
              the interval
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [a,b]
              is connected. By example
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ContinuousImagesOfConnectedSpacesAreConnected"}
              also its image
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f([a,b]) \subset \mathbb{R}
              is connected. By example
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ConnectedSubspacesOfRealLineAreTheIntervals"}
              that image is hence itself an interval. This implies the claim.
          #ClosureOfConnectedSubspaceIsConnected.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_4 Proposition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+closure"} topological closure
                of connected subspace is connected)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi τ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\tau)
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subset X
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
              which, as a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspace"} subspace
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+closure"} topological closure
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl(S) \subset X
              is connected.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_11 Proof
            %p
              Suppose that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊔
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl(S) = A \sqcup B
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B \subset X
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+subsets"} disjoint
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
            %p
              But also the intersections
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi S
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cap S\,,B \cap S \subset S
              are disjoint subsets, open as subsets of the subspace
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              with
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∩
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊔
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo ∩
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S = (A \cap S) \sqcup (B \cap S)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cap S
              or
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \cap S
              is empty. Assume
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \cap S
              is empty, otherwise rename. Hence
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∩
                      %mi S
                      %mo =
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cap S = S
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subset A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              this means that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi Cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∖
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subset Cl(S) \setminus B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              is open,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∖
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl(S) \setminus B
              is still closed, so that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi Cl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∖
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Cl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi Cl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∖
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (S \subset Cl(S) \setminus B)
                    \\Rightarrow
                    (Cl(S) \subset Cl(S) \setminus B)
                    \\,.
            %p
              This means that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo =
                      %mi ∅
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B = \emptyset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl(S)
              is connected.
          #ConnectedComponentsAreClosed.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_5 Proposition
            %p
              %strong (connected components are closed)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi τ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\tau)
              be a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ComponentsConnected"}
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subsets"} closed subsets
          .proof
            %h6#proof_12 Proof
            %p
              By definition, the connected components are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maximal+elements"} maximal elements
              in the set of connected subspaces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorder"} pre-ordered
              by inclusion. By prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ClosureOfConnectedSubspaceIsConnected"}
              this means that they must contain their closures, hence they must equal their closures.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_4 Remark
            %p
              Prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ConnectedComponentsAreClosed"}
              implies that when a space has a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite set
              of connected components, then they are not just closed but also open, hence
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/clopen+subsets"} clopen subsets
              (because then each is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complement
              of a finite union of closed subspaces).
            %p
              For a non-finite set of connected components this remains true if the space is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+connected+topological+space"} locally connected
              %a{:href => "locally+connected+topological+space#AlternativeCharacterizationsOfLocalConnectivity"} this prop.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+connected+topological+space"} locally connected topological space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+topos"}> connected topos
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+connected+topos"} locally connected topos
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"} cohesive topos
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible+topological+space"} contractible topological space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/almost+connected+topological+group"} almost connected topological group
            %li
              %p
                A space in which the only connected subspaces are the singletons and the empty set is called
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/totally+disconnected+space"} totally disconnected space
            %li
              %p
                The connected subsets of a space form a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connectology"} connectology
          %p
            %br/
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply+connected+topological+space"} simply connected topological space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+topological+space"} simple topological space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nilpotent+topological+space"} nilpotent topological space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+connected+space"} compact connected space
          %h2#references References
          %p Examples of countable connected Hausdorff spaces were give in
          %ul
            %li#Bing
              %p R.H. Bing, A connected countable Hausdorff space, Proc. Amer. Math. Soc. 4 (1953), 474.
            %li#Golomb
              %p
                Solomon W. Golomb,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Connected Topology for the Integers
          %p
            Discussion of connectedness in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            is in
          %ul
            %li#Taylor10
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Taylor"}> Paul Taylor
              , Def. 13.2 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em A lambda calculus for real analysis
              %strong 2
              5 (2010) 1–115
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://logicandanalysis.org/index.php/jla/article/view/63/25"} L&amp;A:63/25
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://logicandanalysis.org/index.php/jla/article/download/63/25"} pdf
              %a{:href => "https://www.paultaylor.eu/ASD/lamcra/"} webpage
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
          %p
            Related discussion in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} cohesive homotopy type theory
          %ul
            %li#Shulman18
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
              , Thm. 11.1, 11.3
              = succeed "," do
                %em Brouwer’s fixed-point theorem in real-cohesive homotopy type theory
              %strong 28
              6 (2018) 856-941
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.07584"} arXiv:1509.07584
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0960129517000147"} doi:10.1017/S0960129517000147
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
          %p Material on arc-connected spaces and the Hahn-Mazurkiewicz theorem can be found in Chapter 31 of
          %ul
            %li#Willard
              Stephen Willard,
              = succeed "," do
                %em General Topology
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.scribd.com/doc/132023793/44283132-General-Topology-Stephen-Willard#scribd"} online
          %p Material on pseudo-arcs can be found in
